  "summary_text": "Kyle, Port Washington delivered solid execution yesterday despite running on skeleton crew\u2014one groomer generated $385, which is 18% below our trailing 30-day baseline of $469, but that's a capacity story, not a performance story. What matters: your team converted 100% add-on attach (up from 60% last week) and crushed retail (+71%), meaning front-of-house discipline is working. Month-to-date, Port is tracking $48k vs. a DOW-adjusted expectation of $53.6k\u2014that $5.6k miss is real performance loss, not calendar drift, pointing to staffing gaps earlier this month that squeezed bookings. Year-to-date you're up 37% revenue and 36% appointments, so the business is structurally strong; you need to plug the MTD capacity hole.\n\nHicksville is the bright spot: up 9% MTD (3.4% after DOW adjustment) on flat groomer deployment, and $/groomer-day sits at $446 vs. $384 last month\u2014your team is running hotter. Yesterday's zero revenue was a Sunday miss (no grooming scheduled), not a trend. Across both locations, you're sitting on 709 dormant-or-lapsed customers worth $457k annually; that's low-hanging retention upside if you can execute a reactivation campaign.\n\nOne operational concern: both locations have 20+ items out of stock, led by X-Small Treat Table (critical at Hicksville with negative inventory). Reorder immediately or you'll leave retail dollars on the table. Glen Cove remains a retail-only placeholder with zero grooming capacity and no recovery plan visible.\n\nPort Washington: 5 groomer(s) today, 6 tomorrow; Hicksville: 1 today, 3 tomorrow; combined 36 groomer-days scheduled over next 7 days with 83 bather-hours should drive mid-week recovery if bookings hold."
